1. What is CI/CD?
CI/CD stands for:
a. Continuous Integration (CI)
  ->Developers regularly merge code changes into a shared repository. Automated testing validates changes before integration.
b. Continuous Delivery/Deployment (CD)
  ->Validated code is automatically prepared and deployed to testing or production environments.
Benefits of CI/CD
* Faster software delivery
* Early bug detection
* Improved software quality
* Reduced deployment risk
* Better collaboration
* Reliable releases
CI/CD helps teams deliver software safely and efficiently.

2. Why Deployment Workflow Matters?
  ->A deployment workflow defines how changes move from development to production.
Typical Workflow
1. Developer writes code
2. Code committed to GitHub
3. Automated tests run
4. Validation checks performed
5. Deployment to testing environment
6. User acceptance testing
7. Production deployment
 Benefits
* Controlled releases
* Reduced downtime
* Improved reliability
* Easier troubleshooting
* Better team coordination
Without a deployment workflow, software releases become risky and difficult to manage.

3.Enterprise Deployment Risks
 ->Why Directly Editing Production is Dangerous
  ->Suppose the College Management System serves:
* 50,000 Students
* 500 Faculty Members
* Multiple Administrators
  ->Direct changes in production can cause serious problems.
a.Bugs
  ->A coding error may break registration or attendance systems.
b. Downtime
  ->Users may be unable to access critical functionality.
c. Broken Workflows
  ->Automations, Flows, and Apex logic may stop working correctly.
d. Data Loss
  ->Incorrect updates may corrupt or delete important records.
e. Security Risks
  ->Improper changes may expose sensitive information.
  ->For these reasons, enterprise teams never make major changes directly in production.

